===============================================================================
/etc/shells FILE & SHELL CATEGORIES
===============================================================================

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PART 1: TECHNICAL DOCUMENTATION (/etc/shells)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. INTRODUCTION & OVERVIEW
--------------------------
The /etc/shells file is a plain-text configuration file found on Linux and 
Unix-like operating systems. It contains a list of full paths to valid, allowed 
login shells available on the system.

System services, administrative commands, and third-party applications consult 
this file to determine whether a given program or shell is permitted to act as 
an interactive user shell.


2. FILE LOCATION AND DEFAULT CONTENTS
-------------------------------------
* Location: /etc/shells
* Permissions: Usually owned by root, readable by all users (-rw-r--r-- or 644)

Typical Structure:
Each line in /etc/shells contains an absolute path to an executable shell. 
Lines starting with '#' are treated as comments.

# /etc/shells: valid login shells
/bin/sh
/bin/bash
/usr/bin/bash
/bin/rbash
/usr/bin/rbash
/usr/bin/sh
/bin/dash
/usr/bin/dash
/bin/zsh
/usr/bin/zsh


3. KEY FUNCTIONS & DEPENDENCIES
-------------------------------
/etc/shells plays a crucial role across several system utilities:

A. The chsh (Change Shell) Command
   When a user attempts to change their default login shell using the `chsh` 
   command, `chsh` checks the target shell against /etc/shells. If the requested 
   shell path is not listed in /etc/shells, non-root users will be denied 
   the change.

B. Security & System Daemons (FTP, SSH, PAM)
   - FTP Servers (e.g., vsftpd, proftpd): Many FTP daemons disallow logins for 
     users whose default shell (defined in /etc/passwd) is not listed in 
     /etc/shells.
   - PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules): The pam_shells.so module restricts 
     access to services if the user's shell isn't registered in /etc/shells.

C. Restricted Logins (/sbin/nologin)
   To block interactive logins for system accounts (e.g., nobody, www-data, 
   ftp), administrators set their shell to /sbin/nologin or /bin/false.
   /sbin/nologin is often added to /etc/shells if services require a valid 
   shell entry to function, even for non-interactive users.


4. USEFUL COMMANDS FOR WORKING WITH /etc/shells
-----------------------------------------------
A. Display Installed Valid Shells:
   cat /etc/shells
   grep '^[^#]' /etc/shells

B. Add a New Shell to /etc/shells:
   echo "/usr/local/bin/fish" | sudo tee -a /etc/shells

C. Check Your Current Default Shell:
   echo $SHELL

D. Change Default Shell (chsh):
   chsh -s /bin/zsh


5. SECURITY & BEST PRACTICES
----------------------------
1. Always Use Absolute Paths: Ensure every entry in /etc/shells starts with 
   a root directory `/` (e.g., /bin/zsh instead of zsh).
2. Do Not Add Arbitrary Executables: Never add general binaries (like 
   /usr/bin/python3 or custom scripts) unless explicitly intended to act 
   as a restricted login shell wrapper.
3. Handle Symlinks Carefully: Many modern Linux distributions use /usr/bin 
   merge, where /bin/bash is a symlink to /usr/bin/bash. Keep both paths 
   listed in /etc/shells to ensure compatibility across older tools and scripts.


===============================================================================
PART 2: TYPES OF SHELLS, CATEGORIES & EXAMPLES
===============================================================================

1. BOURNE SHELL FAMILY (POSIX-COMPLIANT)
----------------------------------------
* bash (Bourne Again SHell)
  - Paths: /bin/bash, /usr/bin/bash
  - Overview: Standard interactive and scripting shell across most Linux systems.
  - Example Command: bash -c 'echo "Hello from Bash"'

* sh (Bourne Shell / POSIX Shell)
  - Paths: /bin/sh, /usr/bin/sh
  - Overview: The baseline Unix shell. Usually symlinked to bash or dash.
  - Example Command: sh -c 'echo "POSIX standard mode"'

* dash (Debian Almquist Shell)
  - Paths: /bin/dash, /usr/bin/dash
  - Overview: Lightweight, fast shell used for system boot scripts in Debian/Ubuntu.
  - Example Command: dash -c 'echo "Executing via Dash"'

* rbash (Restricted Bash)
  - Paths: /bin/rbash, /usr/bin/rbash
  - Overview: Restricted version of Bash to restrict users from changing directories 
    or modifying system variables.
  - Example Command: rbash


2. C SHELL FAMILY (C-STYLE SYNTAX)
----------------------------------
* csh (C Shell)
  - Paths: /bin/csh, /usr/bin/csh
  - Overview: Shell designed with syntax similar to the C programming language.

* tcsh (TENEX C Shell)
  - Paths: /bin/tcsh, /usr/bin/tcsh
  - Overview: Enhanced version of csh with command-line completion and line editing.
  - Example Command: tcsh -c 'echo "Hello from TCSH"'


3. KORN SHELL FAMILY
--------------------
* ksh (KornShell)
  - Paths: /bin/ksh, /usr/bin/ksh
  - Overview: Combines Bourne shell syntax with C shell features, offering 
    advanced math and associative arrays.
  - Example Command: ksh -c 'print "Hello from Ksh"'


4. ADVANCED INTERACTIVE SHELLS
------------------------------
* zsh (Z Shell)
  - Paths: /bin/zsh, /usr/bin/zsh
  - Overview: Advanced shell with powerful tab-completion, themes, and plugins.
    Default shell on modern macOS.
  - Example Command: zsh -c 'echo "Running in Zsh"'

* fish (Friendly Interactive Shell)
  - Paths: /usr/bin/fish, /usr/local/bin/fish
  - Overview: Modern shell focused on out-of-the-box auto-suggestions and syntax 
    highlighting.
  - Example Command: fish -c 'echo "Hello from Fish"'


5. NON-INTERACTIVE & SECURITY SHELLS
------------------------------------
* nologin / false
  - Paths: /sbin/nologin, /bin/false
  - Overview: Used to prevent service accounts (e.g., www-data, ftp) from 
    logging in interactively.
